WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:40.880
The discovery of oil has revived Venezuela's&nbsp;
claims to the so-called Guayana Esequiba,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:45.680
the territory west of the Essequibo River. This&nbsp;
is no less than two thirds of present-day Guyana.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:49.200
It is not surprising that Guyana has&nbsp;
offered good conditions to both the US&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.240
giant ExxonMobil and the Chinese State&nbsp;
corporation in order to get them on its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:56.360
side in this conflict. So everything&nbsp;
indicates that Guyana will be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.360 --> 00:01:00.240
to enjoy unprecedented wealth thanks to&nbsp;
the oil discovered by this consortium.
 &nbsp;

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.240
Now the question is, how will they use this&nbsp;
enormous wealth? How much potential money are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:09.360
we talking about exactly?
 
Well, let's take a look.&nbsp;

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:16.400
(GIANT)
 
It seems, VisualPolitik viewers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:20.240
that the oil reserves discovered are so&nbsp;
large that they promise to provide Guyana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:27.560
with – hold on to your chair – approximately $160&nbsp;
billion over the next 20 years. Check it out.
 &nbsp;

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:31.600
It will undoubtedly be a massive change&nbsp;
for a country where more than 40% of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.640
800,000 inhabitants live in abject&nbsp;
poverty on less than $5.50 a day.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:39.560
And we are not talking about a&nbsp;
distant dream. In 2022 alone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:46.960
government revenues from hydrocarbon exploitation&nbsp;
will exceed US$1 billion. That is about US$1,250&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:51.600
per capita. Almost 90% of the government's&nbsp;
ordinary income in the preceding years.
 &nbsp;

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:55.400
Guyana will now be able to realize its&nbsp;
goals and become a modern and prosperous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.400 --> 00:02:00.120
country. For example, one of the dreams is to take&nbsp;
advantage of its rivers to build a hydroelectric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:09.040
power plant that will provide the nation with&nbsp;
clean and stable energy. The ideal location has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:15.480
been identified since 1976 and since 1997, the&nbsp;
Government of Guyana has had plans to build it.
 &nbsp;

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:18.800
However, throughout all the intervening&nbsp;
years there was one thing missing:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:25.240
the $840 million it will cost to build the Amaila&nbsp;
Falls project or someone interested in putting up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:30.040
all the dough in exchange for its exploitation.
 
But now, in theory at least, that problem is about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:34.800
to disappear: Guyana will be able to finance it&nbsp;
and, in addition, its newly buoyant and growing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.720
economy will create demand for the energy&nbsp;
that would make the operation profitable.
 &nbsp;

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:42.840
Curiously, however, the Chinese company that&nbsp;
was awarded the project has just pulled out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:46.800
This will delay commissioning beyond&nbsp;
2027. It is quite a setback for Guyana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.700
but President Ali maintains that they are&nbsp;
not giving up on the project. Take a look:
 &nbsp;

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:56.200
(VIDEO2: The independent review by Norway has&nbsp;
pointed to this project as being good for Guyana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:59.800
being good for the environment&nbsp;
and bringing greater prosperity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:03.280
So this project will get under way)
 
It is precisely because of all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:07.880
reasons that Ali has just pointed out that what&nbsp;
is puzzling is that this project is not going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:12.360
be undertaken immediately, even if it is through&nbsp;
the State electricity company. But I suppose it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:20.400
will be a matter of budgetary prudence. If that&nbsp;
is the reasoning... Then it is not so bad.
 &nbsp;

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:26.200
In any case, a 227-kilometer gas pipeline to&nbsp;
the coast and a natural gas combined cycle plant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:30.840
in the vicinity of Georgetown are already under&nbsp;
construction. ExxonMobil itself will be in charge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:35.200
of this, which will subtract the cost from the&nbsp;
profits accruing to Guyana. However, what there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:44.560
also money for, as always when the Indo-Guyanese&nbsp;
govern, is the same as always, a real black hole.
 &nbsp;

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:49.080
Since coming to power in August 2020, Irfaan&nbsp;
Ali's government has made several capital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:53.840
injections into the state-owned GuySuCo sugar&nbsp;
company, which is having trouble even paying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:58.000
salaries. It is even working to reopen&nbsp;
loss-making plants simply because they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:02.360
employment niches for Indo-Guyanese. In the&nbsp;
last two years alone, public aid granted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:11.920
GuySuCo has amounted to approximately 30 billion&nbsp;
Guyanese dollars, or about $140 million dollars.
 &nbsp;

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:16.000
Of course, with all the flood of money expected,&nbsp;
these seem like nothing more than crumbs.
 &nbsp;

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:19.480
In any case Guyana is going to&nbsp;
change a lot over the next few years:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:23.800
roads, ports, schools, hospitals.....
Guyana has become the fastest growing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:30.720
economy in the world. We are talking about 44%&nbsp;
in 2020 and 20% in 2021. By 2022 the government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:36.360
expects 48% growth. And even if we take oil out of&nbsp;
the equation, growth almost reaches 8%. Things are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:41.040
going better than ever... At all levels.
 
N4 (Govt partners with Mount Sinai,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:45.640
Hess Corp to transform Guyana's healthcare)
 
Hess Corporation, one of Guyana's three oil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:50.080
companies, is a major donor to New York's Mount&nbsp;
Sinai Hospital, one of the most prestigious in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:53.240
the world, and has been influential&nbsp;
in getting the organization involved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.240 --> 00:05:01.240
in modernizing Guyana's healthcare system.
 
Other positive news coming from the consortium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:05.200
is the driving effect that its activity has&nbsp;
on Guyana's industry. It is true that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:09.120
could be greater, because there are areas that&nbsp;
local companies are currently unable to reach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:13.880
as the country has so far lacked heavy&nbsp;
industry. But there is something. Take a look.
 &nbsp;

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:19.240
N5(Guyanese suppliers profit from&nbsp;
ExxonMobil's US$540M expenditure)
 &nbsp;

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:24.520
So $540 million may not seem much considering&nbsp;
ExxonMobil's total spending on this project.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:30.640
But it is not bad relative to Guyana's GDP&nbsp;
in 2021, which was $6.5 billion. What’s more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:35.440
ExxonMobil's success in Guyana leaves the country&nbsp;
in a better position to negotiate more beneficial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:40.040
terms with future investors because no one doubts&nbsp;
the potential of Guyana's oilfields anymore.
 &nbsp;

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:43.240
However, the country's ability to attract&nbsp;
investment will depend on its ability to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:47.920
offer legal certainty. And that requires political&nbsp;
stability. If they succeed, Guyana could become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:52.360
something like the Monaco of Latin America.
 
But now the question is over to you: What future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:56.080
do you foresee for Guyana? What do you think it&nbsp;
should do with the oil money? You can leave me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:00.200
your answer in the comments. As always, if you&nbsp;
found the video interesting, please like it and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:13.800
don't forget to subscribe to VisualPolitik.&nbsp;
All the best and see you next time.
 

